<p>This just never quite coalesces for me.</p>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;show&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>The O5s are dead certain of this thing's ultimate intentions, or at least impact. That isn't necessarily a bad thing.<br />
It pumps out toxic creatures that seriously harm humans, and there are so many of them that it will force evolution. Not necessarily a bad thing.<br />
It does strange things to humans. Definitely not a bad thing, although the body horror was <em>way</em> less interesting than them just being there. The best moments on that front were the corrupted D-Class drowning and the Foundation employee on the beach and air bubble, except before I read the specifics in the footnote. The reappearance of Lockwood seemed to ultimately just exist to provide atmosphere, ang it dragged a lot for that.<br />
All of the characters represented in dialogue or writing seemed super concerned with protocol and safety and having solid evidence. Not necessarily a bad thing, a bold choice in fact, but it led to a lot of drag.<br />
Weird incorporeal being fixing stuff? Quite cool, in fact, although the memetic/possession thing got muddled.<br />
Ancient hieroglyphs that sound vaguely like containment procedures? Not new, but definitely not necessarily a bad thing.</p>
<p>But where does it all go? It just reads like this post-mortem, this documentation of a giant manhunt that consistently finds nothing conclusive except for one big dread answer. (I honestly don't know what they expect -2000 to accomplish if the whole biosphere changes.)</p>
<p>There is some horror in futility, and the tragic flaws built into an organisation by their structure and ethos, but the actions here read with their own unique tone compared to other SCPs, and not knowing at all just wasn't satisfying.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

