														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2777051">
									

<p>This is meaty, both in terms of the scientific detail, and the substance of the logs (for which I can appreciate the focus on safety and process, despite the difference in tone they create). There are also some good creepy details - the first half of the exploration, the description of Lockwood's return and the D-Class drowning, in particular.</p>
<p>For me, the article as a whole didn't do enough to drive home these strengths. Part of that comes from the fact that the Containment Procedures give away the ending (the world is toast), reducing the tension of &quot;will the Foundation scrape through&quot;, but the rest of the document doesn't emphasise the horror of failure / inevitable doom to make up for it. Partly as well I think this ends with some of the weaker material - we go from a very unsettling exploration, and the great description of the &quot;strings of color&quot;, to a relatively unsurprising translation, some short exploration vignettes that don't add much to the story other than complicating the anomaly further, a few lesions and heart attacks, and ultimately a confirmation of the explanation that could be intuited from the outset. That summary sounds facetious (as summaries can sometimes sound, and for which I apologise) and the individual parts are still well-written, but they felt less like propulsive additions to the overall narrative than additional loose ends.</p>
<p>I suspect that a few more big revisions to this could produce something very strong.</p>

