<p><strong>1</strong>)</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Agent Jones was equipped with standard two-way radio equipment for the purposes of communicating with observation personnel. All three agents were equipped with full-body locking restraint harnesses attached to a cord rappelling system, &#8230;</p>
<p>Due to transmissions complications caused by the underwater depth of SCP-3000 and the unknown composition of SCP-3000's exterior, real-time video transmission could not be established.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Since they already have cables attached, rather than using radio it'd make more sense for the cables to include wires or fiber-optics for both voice communication and to stream back the video feeds. I can understand why you'd want no real-time video for narrative reasons, but this just feels off.</p>
<p><strong>2</strong>) Any scientific analysis of the unknown lifeforms would include analyzing their DNA to see the differences and similarities to known species.</p>
<p><strong>3</strong>) The article mentions &quot;forced/accelerated evolution&quot;, but that would imply changing the DNA of species already present on Earth. What seems to be happening is the <em>replacement</em> of existing species with new species.</p>
<p><strong>4</strong>) The article doesn't seem to specify why evacuation of Atlantic coastal areas would be needed, or why it would eventually lead to human extinction. Sulfurous fumes released by the ocean making the air unbreathable?</p>
