														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2777491">
									

<p>I appreciate the path taken here of focusing on the effects of the anomaly rather than on its intentions. Explanation isn't a must, and I suspect that doing so might not add much, and could possibly detract from the unsettling nature of the SCP. I feel that the thrust of the article is in the Foundation's attitude to the implications of an end-of-the-world scenario; seeing the use of SCP-2000 as part of the containment procedures in such an as-per-protocol manner is a very strong opener. As other reviewers have expressed, however, there isn't ever really a moment of dreaded revelation or anything that truly stands out as climactic. This feels like a slow-burn horror piece that ends in a quiet, somewhat contemplative fizzle.</p>
<p>There are certainly some memorable bits, in particular the encounter with the entity in the first exploration log (which as a whole is exemplary). There are similarly vivid encounters elsewhere in the piece, but it feels unsatisfying that each individual aspect of the anomaly (the entities, the light, and interior itself) gets so little screen-time. It's almost agonising, because what little is explicitly revealed is golden.</p>

							</div>

								
					
